= Software — Technical =

== Artifact boundary ==
"Software" is not one universally fixed artifact set. Standards and engineering contexts may include programs, procedures, data, configuration, and documentation to different degrees.

A technical claim should therefore name the [[System boundary/Technical|system boundary]] and configuration items in scope.

== Architecture ==
Software architecture concerns the organization of components, interfaces, responsibilities, dependencies, data flows, deployment structures, and quality constraints.

Architectural choices affect:
* modifiability;
* reliability;
* security;
* performance;
* scalability;
* testability;
* deployability.

== Lifecycle ==
A software lifecycle can include:
* requirements;
* design/architecture;
* implementation;
* verification/validation;
* packaging/release;
* deployment;
* operation;
* maintenance/evolution;
* retirement.

Real organizations combine these activities iteratively rather than following one universal linear sequence.

== Configuration and provenance ==
A reproducible software state can depend on:
* source revision;
* dependency graph;
* build tools;
* compiler/runtime versions;
* configuration;
* generated artifacts;
* deployment environment;
* external services.

[[Version control/Technical|Version control]] records only part of this state.

== Quality attributes ==
Software quality cannot be reduced to functional correctness. Relevant attributes can include safety, security, availability, performance, usability, portability, interoperability, maintainability, and observability.

Trade-offs are contextual; improving one quality can degrade another.

== Essential and accidental complexity ==
Brooks distinguished complexity inherent in the conceptual problem from accidental complexity introduced by representation or tooling. The distinction is qualitative, but it remains useful when assessing whether a technology removes incidental work or changes the underlying problem.

